                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "2ee6ad66-f76f-3875-93bf-e9f648613ed6",
                        "entry_id": "2a552843-7497-3c66-8cfc-15b291d2c56f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The act or process of denaturalizing, of changing or destroying the quality (nature) of a thing."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "8e114886-46f0-456c-a30a-b7a0adedf35a",
                                "sense_id": "2ee6ad66-f76f-3875-93bf-e9f648613ed6",
                                "text": "In each feature of this sovereign friendship (exception, improbable and random unicity, metapolitical transcendence, disproportion, infinite dissymmetry, denaturalization, etc.), it might be tempting to recognize a rupture with Greek philía – a testamentary rupture, as some would hasten to conclude, a palaeo- or neo-testamentary rupture."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "35254cac-0e50-35dd-aa1e-e7e0ebd39ea2",
                        "entry_id": "2a552843-7497-3c66-8cfc-15b291d2c56f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The involuntary removal of citizenship by a country"
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "e3376954-9b10-40eb-9595-f96a0d8143cd",
                                "sense_id": "35254cac-0e50-35dd-aa1e-e7e0ebd39ea2",
                                "text": "The Justice Department has long pursued denaturalization on the basis of fraud. A 2016 Homeland Security Department Inspector General report found that at least 858 people had been given US citizenship despite having been deported under a different identity, because their fingerprints had not been in the system."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9a879f8c-89e9-4daf-a0f1-ef7606a4abc3",
                                "sense_id": "35254cac-0e50-35dd-aa1e-e7e0ebd39ea2",
                                "text": "Still, denaturalizations are rare and can only occur in federal court. The Justice Department has filed 228 civil denaturalization cases since 2008, according to a DOJ official. Of the 228 cases, 94 were filed over roughly the last three years, indicating a recent jump in filings, the official said."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
